WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.862
A Dockerfile is part of the application security surface because it decides what enters the image and what remains available at runtime.

2
00:00:08.862 --> 00:00:21.191
It also affects repeatability, build provenance, package restore behavior, user identity, working directory, file permissions, environment variables, and operational expectations.

3
00:00:21.191 --> 00:00:26.122
Multi-stage builds help separate build activity from production runtime.

4
00:00:26.122 --> 00:00:33.597
The build stage can use an SDK image to restore packages, compile code, run allowed build steps, and publish output.

5
00:00:33.597 --> 00:00:40.840
The runtime stage can then copy only the published application and the runtime dependencies needed to operate.

6
00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:45.078
That separation reduces unnecessary content in the final image.

7
00:00:45.078 --> 00:00:48.931
The build context deserves the same care as source control.

8
00:00:48.931 --> 00:01:00.721
A . dockerignore file should exclude secrets, local configuration, test data, build output, temporary files, user-specific files, and anything else the image build does not need.

9
00:01:00.721 --> 00:01:07.116
Sending less context to the builder reduces accidental exposure and makes builds easier to audit.

10
00:01:07.116 --> 00:01:09.967
Build-time secrets need special handling.

11
00:01:09.967 --> 00:01:18.521
Ordinary build arguments and build-time environment variables can be recorded in image history, layers, logs, or cache metadata.

12
00:01:18.521 --> 00:01:29.155
If a build truly needs access to a private feed or signing material, use platform-supported build-secret mechanisms and review what appears in logs and artifacts.

13
00:01:29.155 --> 00:01:42.254
The final image should not contain package caches, test output, source code, local development files, package feed credentials, signing keys, or extra tools that are not part of production operation.

14
00:01:42.254 --> 00:01:48.727
CI logs should avoid tokens, connection strings, private feed credentials, and signing material.

15
00:01:48.727 --> 00:01:56.279
The build pipeline should produce a runtime image that is small enough to inspect and predictable enough to rebuild.
